Dear Netflix executives, I hope this memo finds you well. After acquiring and breaking down a dataset for Netflix that covered data from the first half of 2023 up to the first half of 2026. This seven semi-annual time window gave us a lot of insight regarding the company’s trajectory and numbers., especially important statistics like runtime, views, and hours viewed. Taking a look at the data provided, I can confidently say that the decision to cut down the total title catalog from 18,162 from 2023’s first half to 6,595 in 2023’s second half was successful. A lot of titles in the catalog did not gather much attraction and viewership and were rightfully taken out of the platform. This 63.7% cut down resulted in cutting off dead weight and saving costs of licenses for shows that weren’t game changes for our revenue. On a very positive note, this cut down had very quick effects on the total runtime increased by 14.64%! In terms of the long term numbers, from the second half to the last semi-annual period covered by the dataset, total runtime increased by another 25.42%! The catalog may have increased by 24.14% ( 6,595 to 8,187 ), however, it shows us that the titles acquired during that period had a positive effect on viewership and engagement. A huge catalog will consist of titles that the average viewer may not know or won’t bother taking a look at. It’s always important to prioritize tactics that will maximize our revenue and limit our costs. Now, let’s move on to our most important asset, our Netflix Original productions. After breaking down the data provided, our top 5 titles in terms of total % viewer watch time were all Netflix originals. The Night Agent: Season 1 that topped the first half of 2023 had the lowest total watch time percentage with 0.87. The huge catalog that Netflix had at the time may have skewed its percentage negatively. I’m making this observation because when the catalog size was cut down, the hourly views increased by 18.72% between 2023’s Night Agent: Season 1 (812,100,000 hours ) and Wednesday: Season 2 (964,100,000 hours). We want our viewers to see what makes us different compared to the rest of the competition. Exclusive content is what makes us stand out and our recent TV shows were all major hits. From Bridgerton, Squid Game, Wednesday, and especially the global phenomenon Stranger Things. These statistics also show us that we should invest in more TV shows rather than movies. 30.8 % of the total catalog during the first half of 2023 were made up of movies. That number dropped to 4.2% by the second half of 2023. Despite this drastic change in numbers, Netflix’s engagement increased. I’m sorry to say this, but movies are not our thing. Sure, we can invest in movies like The Irishman, a production with heavy hitter protagonists and a highly acclaimed producer like Martin Scorsese from time to time, but the majority of our movie productions have proven to be flops. As part of our strategy, we should allocate our budget towards TV shows which have proven to make us stand out in the market. Competitors like Disney have the upper hand when it comes to movies because they usually release movies in cinemas before moving them to their streaming platform. Their history with the box office has lasted for almost a century and it would be hard to compete with them as they have developed a stronger bond with their audience when it comes to movie productions. If we decide to invest more money into our movie productions and catalog, we can try to spend money and buy franchises (which would cost us a lot of money) just like Disney did with Star Wars and Marvel. Although this could be an option, the risk is much greater. We should instead take advantage of our successful TV show productions, and make investments there as they are offered globally. The One Piece live-action has shown great numbers so far and brings more people to our platform. Lastly, I would like to discuss our limited live event offerings. I thi